  "surprise": "748 at 8 loops (755/761/728) against 507's 732 (746/765/684): +16, keep by the rule, not shown beyond noise. Seeds 0 and 1 moved +9 and -4; the mean gain is seed 2 (+44), where 507's seed had a weak start (1-loop acceptance 0.040, 0.145, 0.214, 0.273 over rounds 1-4) and only 9 rounds, while this run's seed 2 started at 0.133 and got 11 (11/11/11; 507: 11/11/9). The mechanism did what was predicted and bought less than predicted. Fit: last-round 1-loop acceptance on RL targets 0.734/0.731/0.736 (507: 0.716/0.703/0.678; predicted 0.72-0.78), and from round 4 on the Muon fine-tune is ahead of 507 at every round on seed 0 by 0.01-0.04 (0.604 0.638 0.667 0.676 0.695 0.708 0.707 0.734 against 0.587 0.616 0.628 0.671 0.686 0.687 0.699 0.716); round-2 acceptance 0.368/0.441/0.426 (507: 0.408/0.319/0.145; predicted 0.42-0.55, seed 0 under it). Per-sample dev accuracy rose on every seed at both depths: 0.492/0.492/0.471 at 8 loops (507: 0.480/0.483/0.445) and 0.465/0.464/0.440 at 2 (507: 0.445/0.445/0.417). Yet cumulative RL solved is 1,265/1,260/1,246 (507: 1,265/1,264/1,208), identical on seeds 0 and 1: the better-fitting model found the same targets, and pass@64 at 8 loops moved at lengths 7-8 (seed 0: 123/124 against 112/121) and not at 9+ (380/94/26/6 against 379/100/25/7), seed 1 mixed (-16 at length 8, +6 at 9). The converse of 507: that run raised coverage with flat per-sample accuracy; this one raises per-sample accuracy with flat coverage, because the proofs it samples more reliably are ones 64 attempts already found. Loops, unchanged by design and in fact: metric minus diag +21 (25/24/13; 507: +18 = 20/22/10), per-sample 8-vs-2 gap 0.027/0.028/0.031 (507: 0.035/0.038/0.028), retries pooled 11.8/14.7/15.8% cracked at 2/4/8 loops (507: 11.0/13.5/14.1), rounds 3+ 10.1/11.9/12.7 (507: 11.5/13.1/13.3), per-sample retry acceptance 0.036/0.053/0.062 (507: 0.033/0.051/0.057); the speculative Muon-on-refinement-directions effect (gap above +28 on two seeds) did not appear. Cost as benched: fine-tune time per round 54-73 s, mean 66/66/67 s (507: 65/64/59), so the graphed Muon step adds 1-2 s a round; ft_steps 588-762; RL charged 1,650/1,690/1,747 s with 150/110/53 s left; no forgetting signal (held-out greedy after RL 0.985/0.986/0.985). Pretraining is the same code (MuonStep is pretraining's capture code moved; bench 13.1-13.9 steps/s as the incumbent): 3,429/3,428/3,351 steps, val at 2 loops 0.0872/0.0877/0.0834 against 507's 0.0815/0.0868/0.0780, worse by 0.006/0.001/0.005, inside the spread the unchanged code shows across runs (503: 0.089/0.085/0.084), and depth-flat within 0.0003 from 2 to 8 loops as always. Interpretation, not established: with up to 4 proofs per target weighted x4 and two passes over 76,800 records, the fine-tune already fits the found proofs about as well as the data allows, so a better optimizer sharpens the policy on them (per-sample accuracy up, the 1-loop pass stronger) without widening what 64 eval attempts reach; 3e-3 was not too hot, and a higher Muon lr or momentum kept across rounds is untested. 85 patch lines (+54/-31), about 35 of them the helper extraction. Next: the RL bottleneck is now what the fine-tune is fed, not how it is fit. A run that spends the leftover 50-150 s per seed and the cheaper rounds on more attempts for the 235-254 targets never solved, or that raises the 1-loop sampling temperature to buy diversity, tests that directly; the loop gap has not moved in nine experiments and still needs a loss that only deep passes can lower.",
